{
  "source_commit": "ba20a2c292",
  "harness_commit": "ba20a2c292",
  "measurement_scope": "Same counting harness and workload as write-amplification-baseline.json (zig build lite-write-amplification, Debug), run after WS3 (storage/docstore.zig and storage/lite/docstore.zig commitStaged()/Ticket split; mutation_commit.zig stages the primary write under the DB apply lock and waits on its ticket only after the lock releases). This harness counts durable syncs/write calls/bytes/commits attributed by Stage and by calling_thread vs background; it does not measure latency or writer-to-writer contention (see known_limitations and README.md).",
  "known_limitations": [
    "indexed_8_writers' per-stage totals are numerically identical to the single-writer indexed case for the same reason noted in write-amplification-baseline.json: the harness counts operations, not latency, so it cannot show the WS3 throughput claim (concurrent writers sharing one group-commit fsync) by itself. There is also no primary_only_8_writers mode in this harness: WS3's improvement is specifically about contention/latency under concurrent writers, which belongs in scripts/bench/lite_sqlite_comparison.py (the throughput table in lite-single-commit-write-path.md's header) or a dedicated capi-level throughput test, neither of which this session completed a clean run of under heavy concurrent load from sibling worktrees' test/build processes on this machine during this session.",
    "primary_only calling_thread_syncs_per_update dropped from 3.0 (baseline receipt) to 2.0 here. That is WS1's native-commit-protocol work (dropping the redundant hint sync), already on this branch's integration base, not a WS3 change: WS3 does not change how many fsyncs one write costs, only whether independent writers' fsyncs are forced to serialize behind the DB apply lock and the capi connection gate.",
    "The capi-level in-process writer lane (capi/db.zig HandleGuard / capi/lite_connection.zig Connection.acquire/refresh/QueuedMutex) described in WS3's scope was not implemented this session -- see the handback report for why and what's needed. mutation_commit.zig's apply-lock release-before-wait (committed at ba20a2c292) is real and is exercised by this harness's primary_commit stage, but connection.mutex and connection.gate.mutex are still held for an entire capi call including the fsync wait, so this harness's capi-level indexed_8_writers mode cannot show a throughput improvement from this session's change alone."
  ],
  "receipt_comparison": "Compare against write-amplification-baseline.json (source_commit 6c0b004e95, primary_only calling_thread_syncs_per_update 3.0, background 12.84; indexed 66.84/background 254.6). This run (ba20a2c292): primary_only 2.0/8.5, indexed 32.56/75.28, indexed_8_writers 32.56/77.65. The indexed numbers are substantially lower than the baseline receipt's 66.84 largely because of other workstreams' changes already integrated onto this branch (WS1/WS4-phase-1/WS5/WS6/WS7), not WS3 specifically; WS3 touches only the primary commit's lock-release timing, not index-publication I/O.",
